#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119519 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b022adb-2663-550f-866d-3365bb2d78c9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a Product"}] :question/id #uuid "c5560ec9-32d6-514d-92fa-fbd77500d943" :question/math-academy-id "e-4431" :question/problem "Given that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050601/assets/bcebda30c5b3a878271e607bc77f04612470812126f5916469147d884516dc20.png) find ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050601/assets/e0f9783da49e275b641d727e32506542449fe6be9db636fd1b6cc6007704b500.png)" :question/worked-solution "Given two ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050601/assets/c17e75a26e1dbfa0e89535380e33c9a232a5db7bb2841dec7a03b0fbe861b8c4.png) matrices ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050601/assets/0dffbd47bb64c877dbb545c2c5024e2e50ce9ec516b7a4509d53f77916e8c791.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050601/assets/c40e3405d51242d87b13698d1e715c1b391185890487e3d2a386cb09d3b952e9.png) we have\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050601/assets/aae29aab049034fe50debd00d825e5e37b19de8f1a803dfb098727bbf8afe0e9.png)\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050601/assets/bf7493e53cfed1ef296dcc0f7020d7ceec014af63a0cbee3f8cac5e1a11afd0d.png)\n\nWe can compute the required determinants as follows:\n\n- For the first determinant, we notice that it's upper triangular. Therefore, the determinant is simply the product of the diagonal entries.\n\n- For the second determinant, we can compute it using a Laplace expansion down the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050601/assets/c23fa2f99aef303059a834682e511943ffbe022294cf2d9ac8e72e707316a607.png)st column.\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050601/assets/b968eb1cf6b8a72d8f4b46202ea1a6ab201145ae2531bfe2b5a2049aafae9b28.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119520 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "164c56ea-afbb-5ce4-800a-19002695db76" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of the Inverse of a Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "17487f8f-4974-5aaf-8729-6b1336ca5eaa" :question/math-academy-id "e-4432" :question/problem "Find $|A^{-1}|,$ if  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050601/assets/8a5169c6a418a8ddaa9b3478e535039b253724ae622692ea728b2a94dc112e4a.png)" :question/worked-solution "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n|A^{-1}| = \\dfrac{1}{|A|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $|A|$ using a Laplace expansion across the $3$rd row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A| & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 14 & -1 \\\\ 7 & -6 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-3(-1)^{3+2}|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 7 & -6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-12+7) \\\\  & =-15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\det(A^{-1}) =  \\dfrac{1}{|A|} = -\\dfrac{1}{15}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119521 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e4dd07e-475b-5c89-883b-0e4adcf6a723" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant Using the Linearity of the Determinant"}] :question/id #uuid "d5d020cb-50ee-53f6-9d8b-d384bc9b7999" :question/math-academy-id "e-4433" :question/problem "Given that \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050601/assets/96eacd1217f27ffb734d64453ac3fca3cac6124a781dcd677c898ec4a4317427.png)\nfind \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050601/assets/64009b40661083c1e152b6277f329c6e22a0588fc0b3fd3d08db5e51ace0b27d.png)" :question/worked-solution "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a & b & c+1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}a+0 & b+0 & c+1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants and expanding the second determinant across the $1$st row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a+0 & b+0 & c+1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & ={{|\\begin{aligned}a & b & c \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|}_{⏟}}_{-10}+|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-10+1(-1)^{1+3}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-10-1 \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328541 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b022adb-2663-550f-866d-3365bb2d78c9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "056c84ca-82fc-5af2-b63f-6618e6d6129d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "220" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328543} {:answer/id #uuid "8166199f-1021-5bdd-ad31-d4420d9b14e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "240" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328544} {:answer/id #uuid "90255b74-e153-57a7-bce5-7f42236db6c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "204" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328545} {:answer/id #uuid "36ebd86d-6964-513a-84ac-dfdbe713bf3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328546} {:answer/id #uuid "db4e3ac3-c49a-5b7c-b1b6-8da393e1bac4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-224" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328547}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8166199f-1021-5bdd-ad31-d4420d9b14e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "240" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328544} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f555972-c1a1-5877-a2d6-e4e2d2f91eeb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328542}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7f1e9143-c8f8-54c0-8457-88cd22fe54aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-130484" :question/problem "Given that $Q=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 9 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],$ find $det(Q).$" :question/worked-solution "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\ndet(Q)=det[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 9 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot det[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that both matrices are triangular. Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Q) & =(4\\cdot 5\\cdot (-1))\\cdot (3\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =(-20)\\cdot (-12) \\\\  & =240.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328548 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b022adb-2663-550f-866d-3365bb2d78c9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c016537d-0643-5921-9130-74f20a94e7db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328550} {:answer/id #uuid "612d2c73-6cb0-511d-8a57-bec354ab7767" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328551} {:answer/id #uuid "6d5b7ab2-ddf2-534a-b23f-c1dba6e950c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328552} {:answer/id #uuid "368e8588-2c02-5294-868e-491d5154a765" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-46" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328553} {:answer/id #uuid "320edd97-f714-572d-8ef9-a505055282bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328554}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "320edd97-f714-572d-8ef9-a505055282bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328554} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f7b6aed-802c-5347-b68f-0f0ee9a227c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328549}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "08afd02e-3d89-5b10-9f3c-49890bfd9786" :question/math-academy-id "q-130488" :question/problem "Given that $P=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}8 & 6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],$ find $det(P).$" :question/worked-solution "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\ndet(P)=det[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot det[\\begin{aligned}8 & 6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that both matrices are triangular. Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P) & =(3\\cdot 1\\cdot (-2))\\cdot (8\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =(-6)\\cdot (-8) \\\\  & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328555 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b022adb-2663-550f-866d-3365bb2d78c9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cc8fac3-25a7-5ee6-824f-b9624bf48413" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-216" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328557} {:answer/id #uuid "5919e07b-6961-55a0-b80d-21519c85e696" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "206" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328558} {:answer/id #uuid "035c84ef-4ab8-5852-90bb-631ec7c3dd5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-214" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328559} {:answer/id #uuid "e03ce580-b6aa-550c-81f7-95106e874235" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328560} {:answer/id #uuid "910659b9-b1fc-5e1c-843e-40f14318033a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "126" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328561}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1cc8fac3-25a7-5ee6-824f-b9624bf48413" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-216" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328557} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc7b5bbb-5cc1-529a-becb-9dc4f4a39a29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328556}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c0cb2937-ddab-532b-9dec-67fdc8af98b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-130493" :question/problem "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & 7 \\\\ 4 & 8\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 2\\end{aligned}],$ find $det(A).$" :question/worked-solution "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & 7 \\\\ 4 & 8\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot det[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(40-28)\\cdot (6-24) \\\\  & =12\\cdot (-18) \\\\  & =-216.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328562 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b022adb-2663-550f-866d-3365bb2d78c9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b0e7b2d-eb11-57a0-beb8-9e950053a11f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "206" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328564} {:answer/id #uuid "d791f058-0a5b-513c-85d8-0f4e350af3da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "240" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328565} {:answer/id #uuid "b4c5352e-1db6-5cf4-b56c-8b6590ac1507" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-236" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328566} {:answer/id #uuid "17756b41-1dcc-5225-9340-5df792f6a184" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-260" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328567} {:answer/id #uuid "3335b00e-18a2-5c3a-ab12-2b874cbd0e8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-224" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328568}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17756b41-1dcc-5225-9340-5df792f6a184" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-260" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328567} :answer-field/id #uuid "91472a39-21cc-51cf-b887-7cde1709f5b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328563}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1b36a457-663f-5d9c-955f-14bf5a8349b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-130497" :question/problem "Given that $C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6\\sqrt{3} & 2 \\\\ 5 & 2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}],$ find $det(C).$" :question/worked-solution "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(C) & =det[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot det[\\begin{aligned}6\\sqrt{3} & 2 \\\\ 5 & 2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(8-18)\\cdot (36-10) \\\\  & =(-10)\\cdot 26 \\\\  & =-260.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328569 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "164c56ea-afbb-5ce4-800a-19002695db76" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of the Inverse of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ba78880-c157-5f7b-b178-e5318c3a3878" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{28}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328571} {:answer/id #uuid "45eec511-b3ca-5043-b154-7b4986059a6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328572} {:answer/id #uuid "421d53c2-8fa4-5819-bcdd-5d6c1cf95168" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{38}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328573} {:answer/id #uuid "59330d28-12e7-5915-8536-db42fb4fe73c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328574} {:answer/id #uuid "96f89cdb-1917-5343-8a6c-761d89dc6f86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "421d53c2-8fa4-5819-bcdd-5d6c1cf95168" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{38}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328573} :answer-field/id #uuid "230e779b-f02e-5843-bfaa-01e477300f91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328570}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3214f766-00dc-5cfa-b865-7bcefd20637b" :question/math-academy-id "q-130529" :question/problem "Find $|{A}^{-1}|,$ if $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n|{A}^{-1}|=\\frac{1}{|A|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $|A|$ using a Laplace expansion across the $2$nd column:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A| & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(-1)^{1+2}|\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|+1(-1)^{2+2}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|+(-1)(-1)^{3+2}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2(-12+2)+(9-5)+(-6+20) \\\\  & =20+4+14 \\\\  & =38\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n|{A}^{-1}|=\\frac{1}{|A|}=\\frac{1}{38}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328576 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "164c56ea-afbb-5ce4-800a-19002695db76" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of the Inverse of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b28bbab-146f-5ced-a95a-aba0ad41f079" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{42}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328578} {:answer/id #uuid "bcf34796-3347-54c1-9165-93ea607cbc3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{44}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328579} {:answer/id #uuid "a8ffe1ea-e42b-52bf-8c80-9834fc4bd15e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{23}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328580} {:answer/id #uuid "befd2a12-b120-5c17-ab8d-895c842d60b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{46}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328581} {:answer/id #uuid "d6e88b7a-dc4d-5cc7-8568-7aebf3cb1955" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328582}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "befd2a12-b120-5c17-ab8d-895c842d60b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{46}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328581} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8469fe6-fca4-57b6-a6cd-aef043484f70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328577}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2900652d-7841-519a-9b93-e726b78d2510" :question/math-academy-id "q-130629" :question/problem "Find $|{A}^{-1}|,$ if $A=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 6 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n|{A}^{-1}|=\\frac{1}{|A|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $|A|$ using a Laplace expansion across the $3$rd row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A| & =|\\begin{aligned}-7 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 6 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2\\cdot (-1)^{3+1}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (3+20) \\\\  & =-46\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n|{A}^{-1}|=\\frac{1}{|A|}=-\\frac{1}{46}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328583 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e4dd07e-475b-5c89-883b-0e4adcf6a723" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant Using the Linearity of the Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d34eb67f-af8e-5dd2-ba8d-838c3f16ca9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "154" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328585} {:answer/id #uuid "628db90c-32db-58a3-8d1b-3f68cba26d7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "88" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328586} {:answer/id #uuid "48b30130-0efe-5501-a23c-9769de16805a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "172" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328587} {:answer/id #uuid "61320dad-f672-5332-82cf-6e4839fa769e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "176" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328588} {:answer/id #uuid "88bc76ff-4122-5f8c-8fa5-a085950fe5cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "168" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328589}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61320dad-f672-5332-82cf-6e4839fa769e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "176" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328588} :answer-field/id #uuid "7cd5074e-1652-5417-92eb-19edc90b68d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328584}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e5e42e9e-f64b-5207-a516-46c11818bcb8" :question/math-academy-id "q-130634" :question/problem "$|\\begin{aligned}160 & 184 & 76 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -8 \\\\ 160 & 184 & 78\\end{aligned}|=$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $1$st row of our determinant is almost equal to the $3$rd row. So, we can rewrite the given determinant, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}160 & 184 & 76 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -8 \\\\ 160 & 184 & 78\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}160 & 184 & 76 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -8 \\\\ 160+0 & 184+0 & 76+2\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}160 & 184 & 76 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -8 \\\\ 160+0 & 184+0 & 76+2\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}160 & 184 & 76 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -8 \\\\ 160 & 184 & 76\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}160 & 184 & 76 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first determinant must be zero since it has two identical rows, while the second determinant can be expanded across the $3$rd row, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}160 & 184 & 76 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -8 \\\\ 160 & 184 & 76\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}160 & 184 & 76 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =0+2(-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}160 & 184 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot (160\\cdot 4-184\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 88 \\\\  & =176\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328590 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e4dd07e-475b-5c89-883b-0e4adcf6a723" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant Using the Linearity of the Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "383f20a5-7a55-5b02-85ce-108d5a9f95c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328592} {:answer/id #uuid "e1fda4bc-f423-540d-836b-337cbefce39e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328593} {:answer/id #uuid "85882fee-1fbc-5302-89c1-5906161c86f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328594} {:answer/id #uuid "5d62ae14-c8d1-52c5-a505-cd16e0c60aa1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328595} {:answer/id #uuid "b600be55-aee9-5cd6-a841-dc18a5084556" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328596}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85882fee-1fbc-5302-89c1-5906161c86f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328594} :answer-field/id #uuid "579bee92-2378-59a1-afe0-8f59383180d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328591}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cf8590b5-6e41-579f-a8ef-0e82f7d4ca63" :question/math-academy-id "q-130777" :question/problem "Given that \n$|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ a & b+2\\end{aligned}|=8$,\nfind \n$|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ a+3 & b\\end{aligned}|.$" :question/worked-solution "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ a+3 & b\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ a+3 & (b+2)-2\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ a+3 & (b+2)-2\\end{aligned}| & ={{|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ a & b+2\\end{aligned}|}_{⏟}}_{8}+|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =8+(-6+6) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120362 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e4dd07e-475b-5c89-883b-0e4adcf6a723" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant Using the Linearity of the Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "adba491a-4709-5ca7-b820-a2f899939f99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118648} {:answer/id #uuid "ae89bf6c-5a19-51c4-a9f6-6bc0772de6b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118649} {:answer/id #uuid "dc639648-0f70-5fba-97ee-b4df233f5842" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118650} {:answer/id #uuid "ddc866d5-434c-5121-b817-39c9168e49b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118651} {:answer/id #uuid "7a39c6cd-1a0e-5267-9b55-9a49f5d7ab6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118652}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "adba491a-4709-5ca7-b820-a2f899939f99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118648} :answer-field/id #uuid "18fabfba-cbd9-5a73-9bc5-a1587c20225d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118647}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b66a6d15-0b61-5b61-a152-b57545d60ac8" :question/math-academy-id "q-42581" :question/problem "Given that \n$|\\begin{aligned}a+3 & -5 \\\\ b & 6\\end{aligned}|=-7$,\nfind \n$|\\begin{aligned}a+2 & -5 \\\\ b+4 & 6\\end{aligned}|.$" :question/worked-solution "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a+2 & -5 \\\\ b+4 & 6\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}(a+3)-1 & -5 \\\\ b+4 & 6\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}(a+3)-1 & -5 \\\\ b+4 & 6\\end{aligned}| & ={{|\\begin{aligned}a+3 & -5 \\\\ b & 6\\end{aligned}|}_{⏟}}_{-7}+|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -5 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-7+(-6+20) \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120363 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "164c56ea-afbb-5ce4-800a-19002695db76" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of the Inverse of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8def6a81-a73b-5038-8dd8-26283be60ec2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{37}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118654} {:answer/id #uuid "abefc152-b70b-5f42-90c9-7bb3a5caa203" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118655} {:answer/id #uuid "d763b319-6fbb-59b3-9b99-a8f176803df0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118656} {:answer/id #uuid "5162d688-fd34-514c-bda6-b0fa5e59d201" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{75}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118657} {:answer/id #uuid "58c60eac-888c-57d2-8088-ae828dcef757" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{45}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118658}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abefc152-b70b-5f42-90c9-7bb3a5caa203" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118655} :answer-field/id #uuid "6395fd63-0e74-5a9a-9023-155bde656108" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118653}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e713fddd-f608-51f9-ac5a-f16a9c918c04" :question/math-academy-id "q-42584" :question/problem "Find $|{A}^{-1}|,$ if $A=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -6 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n|{A}^{-1}|=\\frac{1}{|A|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $|A|$ using a Laplace expansion across the $2$nd column:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A| & =|\\begin{aligned}-5 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -6 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1(-1)^{1+2}|\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{aligned}|+1(-1)^{2+2}|\\begin{aligned}-5 & 4 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{aligned}|-2(-1)^{3+2}|\\begin{aligned}-5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(12-12)+(-15+24)+2(10-16) \\\\  & =0+9-12 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n|{A}^{-1}|=\\frac{1}{|A|}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120378 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "164c56ea-afbb-5ce4-800a-19002695db76" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of the Inverse of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96e7f06d-fcda-58d4-b57d-01f96860dd8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118744} {:answer/id #uuid "9aa5754e-1691-5c0b-8dde-ccd15ca4b108" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118745} {:answer/id #uuid "dcfa4d1c-0665-5c42-948f-f789542f27cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118746} {:answer/id #uuid "697956ad-1222-5651-bb01-7d171703f193" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118747} {:answer/id #uuid "7055e8f3-b46a-5b80-be5c-dca69dc1be13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118748}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dcfa4d1c-0665-5c42-948f-f789542f27cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118746} :answer-field/id #uuid "1477d50e-219e-526c-accc-24d675406d60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118743}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f613c1e6-df74-5921-9adf-aac6f2ef23de" :question/math-academy-id "q-43063" :question/problem "Find $det({A}^{-1}),$ if $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(A)}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $det(A):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =12+3 \\\\  & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\ndet({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(A)}=\\frac{1}{15}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328597 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "164c56ea-afbb-5ce4-800a-19002695db76" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of the Inverse of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c1a724e-b8b4-53c1-a3e2-7baf93b7c0bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328599} {:answer/id #uuid "b716e06e-4a24-5843-af7e-358f2616d7e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328600} {:answer/id #uuid "bc5433e1-de65-5379-ada4-bc049d2d0565" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328601} {:answer/id #uuid "77475570-4b7d-5602-9a68-08c094853791" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328602} {:answer/id #uuid "bb44042f-7983-5000-875c-64a5a19556db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328603}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77475570-4b7d-5602-9a68-08c094853791" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328602} :answer-field/id #uuid "d006ba4a-ab13-5e1e-9966-8bf4618bab5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328598}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b12895cf-ad15-5880-9349-2b67efa87d20" :question/math-academy-id "q-43096" :question/problem "Find $det({A}^{-1}),$ if $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(A)}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $det(A):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-12-9 \\\\  & =-21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\ndet({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(A)}=-\\frac{1}{21}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328604 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b022adb-2663-550f-866d-3365bb2d78c9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad2ee9c1-a0c2-5e74-877f-abd6d3375f96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328606} {:answer/id #uuid "7177b11f-4ce0-5724-9deb-8bb98d2ce5ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328607} {:answer/id #uuid "67e23f37-aa47-5f39-884f-b37b43553460" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-58" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328608} {:answer/id #uuid "f0d3a95f-b971-5ae9-b403-0c02e8bbaa00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "754" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328609} {:answer/id #uuid "7d4e7c1e-d2f3-52ca-ab59-db437681c332" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-158" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328610}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67e23f37-aa47-5f39-884f-b37b43553460" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-58" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328608} :answer-field/id #uuid "52eed01f-db55-5139-b1ef-b59df29a27d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fe56f8b4-fcdd-5570-97cc-5e3717b1baf4" :question/math-academy-id "q-88266" :question/problem "Given that $B=[\\begin{aligned}6\\sqrt{2} & -5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}],$ find $det(B).$" :question/worked-solution "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =det[\\begin{aligned}6\\sqrt{2} & -5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot det[\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(24-(-5))\\cdot (-14-(-12)) \\\\  & =29\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-58.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328611 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e4dd07e-475b-5c89-883b-0e4adcf6a723" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant Using the Linearity of the Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7e999aa-c40f-528c-ae7a-b8f52b214345" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328613} {:answer/id #uuid "8cbf9b53-8ae7-5b1d-849a-d27eb92055a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328614} {:answer/id #uuid "341eb0e6-9936-5bb5-8bb6-1f27996b52c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328615} {:answer/id #uuid "4badf198-b999-575f-a489-ec20900ed02c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328616} {:answer/id #uuid "bbf94cb5-180a-5fcf-8f1f-f7828ba20919" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328617}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bbf94cb5-180a-5fcf-8f1f-f7828ba20919" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328617} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2070df0-91a9-5db2-83c5-bfc1db56f83b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328612}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e4e01cea-b173-5958-8b12-dc5e87fd8c79" :question/math-academy-id "q-88296" :question/problem "Given that \n$|\\begin{aligned}a & b & c \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|=4,$\nfind \n$|\\begin{aligned}a+1 & b & c \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|.$" :question/worked-solution "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a+1 & b & c \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}a+1 & b+0 & c+0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants and expanding the second determinant across the $1$st row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a+1 & b+0 & c+0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & ={{|\\begin{aligned}a & b & c \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|}_{⏟}}_{4}+|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4+1(-1)^{1+1}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4+1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328618 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e4dd07e-475b-5c89-883b-0e4adcf6a723" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant Using the Linearity of the Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4229129-d2ae-5362-ae40-11f6ed5591c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328620} {:answer/id #uuid "fd007614-b224-5415-bcb6-a48fa7dd2286" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328621} {:answer/id #uuid "164a46a6-5159-5d59-ac7e-46b14f3ed66d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328622} {:answer/id #uuid "08d4ea76-9159-5f1f-af75-9e5e280e86e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328623} {:answer/id #uuid "058577f6-8bf9-5f85-8c93-282b4fe3eda2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328624}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4229129-d2ae-5362-ae40-11f6ed5591c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328620} :answer-field/id #uuid "b445986a-b1e7-5123-90ec-ab8243f73738" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328619}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "48d2b778-ec09-5e1b-8fc5-141f51e38691" :question/math-academy-id "q-88298" :question/problem "Given that \n$|\\begin{aligned}a+2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ b+3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ c & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|=5,$\nfind \n$|\\begin{aligned}a+1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ b & 4 & 2 \\\\ c & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|.$" :question/worked-solution "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a+1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ b & 4 & 2 \\\\ c & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}(a+2)-1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ (b+3)-3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ c+0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants and expanding the second determinant across the $3$rd row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}(a+2)-1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ (b+3)-3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ c+0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| & ={{|\\begin{aligned}(a+2) & -2 & 2 \\\\ (b+3) & 4 & 2 \\\\ c & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|}_{⏟}}_{5}+|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =5+1\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =5+(-10) \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
